WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Israel's desalination plants solved its own water shortages, but left the lower Jordan saltier and the Dead Sea shrinking. This is unintended consequences, Episode 135. Israeli planners faced recurring droughts and a growing population that placed heavy demands on the Jordan River Basin and the Sea of Galilee. After 2000, the government turned to large-scale seawater desalination as a way to create new supply rather than further restrict use.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.720
The approach promised to meet urban and agricultural needs without drawing additional water

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.900
from already stressed inland sources.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:42.440
Officials viewed the technology as a practical engineering response to physical limits on

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:43.340
natural recharge.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.640
At the time, the policy aligned with existing regional water sharing arrangements that

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.720
focused on allocation volumes rather than on the chemistry or volume of return flows.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:59.260
Decision makers reasonably expected that adding supply at the coast would relieve pressure

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:00.260
throughout the system.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.520
They calculated that each new cubic meter produced on the Mediterranean would substitute

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:11.000
directly for a cubic meter previously taken from the upper basin, lowering the need to open

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.540
gates at upstream reservoirs during dry months.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.760
Because the sea of Galilee had already shown measurable drops in level during successive

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:23.460
drought years, the arithmetic of coastal production appeared to protect that reservoir

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:28.380
as storage, without requiring changes to the treaties that set minimum delivery quantities

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:28.960
to Jordan.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.580
In addition, the planners noted that reverse osmosis membranes could be scaled in modular

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:40.660
trains, allowing incremental additions of capacity rather than a single large project whose financing

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:41.460
might stall.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:47.060
This stepwise expansion matched the pace at which municipal demand was projected to rise,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:52.040
giving the impression that supply growth and ecological protection could advance together.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:57.200
Construction of major reverse osmosis plants began in the first years of the decade,

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:02.540
with facilities at Ashkelon and later Haderah and Saurik coming online in sequence.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:08.220
By the middle of the 2010s, the plant supplied a substantial share of Israel's municipal water.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.140
Agricultural output expanded as treated waste water and desalinated water

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.800
substituted for river withdrawals in some areas.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.480
Proponents highlighted the reliability of the new resource

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.120
course, and the reduction in pumping from the sea of Galilee.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:27.340
Regional agreements with Jordan continue to specify delivery quantities from the upper

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:33.020
basin, without provisions that track changes in downstream water quality, or the volume of

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.320
agricultural return flows once coastal supply increased.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.860
Early performance data showed that the ash-colon plant alone produced roughly 100 million cubic

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:47.900
meters per year, once fully commissioned, a volume that matched a sizable fraction

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:51.280
of the annual allocation previously drawn from the upper Jordan.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:57.160
Farmers in the coastal plain and central highlands began receiving blended desalinated water

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:02.160
through existing pipelines, which in turn allowed them to keep irrigation schedules intact

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.020
even when the sea of Galilee level fell below historic averages.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:12.400
The substitution effect was visible in metering records, with draws from the upper basin declined

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.240
in proportion to the desalinated volumes delivered, yet the treaties themselves remained

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:22.160
silent on whether those reduced withdrawals would alter the salinity of the residual flow that

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:28.140
continued southward. With more water available from the coast, upstream users had less incentive

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:34.280
to release fresh water into the lower Jordan to meet local needs. The reduced volume of relatively

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:39.480
fresh water allowed saline springs and irrigation drainage to dominate the river's chemistry.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:45.580
Salinity levels rose, harming riparian vegetation and fish populations that had adapted to

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:50.300
lower concentrations. The lower flow also accelerated the rate at which the

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:55.220
Dead Sea lost volume because less water reached its northern shore. Second

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.680
order effects appeared in the surrounding wetlands and in the quality of

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:03.380
water available for downstream communities that still relied on the river. Third

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:07.220
order effects emerged when existing water-sharing treaties proved poorly

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:11.700
suited to managing quality rather than quantity, leaving both countries with

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.740
fewer practical options for restoring environmental flows.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:20.920
The causal chain ran from supply augmentation at one point in the basin to altered return

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:26.180
flow patterns at another, and outcome the original infrastructure program had not modeled.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:28.980
Consider the arithmetic of dilution.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:34.780
When the upper Jordan released 300 million cubic meters of low salinity water annually,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:41.160
that volume mixed with roughly 100 million cubic meters of saline, spring discharge and irrigation

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:47.260
return. The resulting mixture stayed below thresholds that stressed most riparian species.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:53.860
Once coastal desalination displaced 150 million cubic meters of that upper basin release,

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:59.580
the same saline load now mixed with only 150 million cubic meters of freshwater,

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:05.660
roughly doubling the concentration downstream. riparian vegetation that had stabilized

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:11.420
banks began to thin, increasing erosion during winter spates. Fish species whose

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:16.120
spawning cycles depended on the former salinity gradient declined, prompting local

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:21.080
fishers to report smaller catches within a decade. The Dead Sea itself received

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:26.120
several tens of millions fewer cubic meters each year, an incremental loss that

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.420
compounded the already negative water balance caused by evaporation and

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:34.640
mineral extraction. Because the treaties measured compliance in delivered

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:39.860
cubic meters rather than in delivered salt load or minimum environmental flow, neither

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:45.600
party had a contractual lever to request compensatory releases once the salinity trend became clear.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:50.660
Once the salinity increase and accelerated Dead Sea decline became measurable,

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:55.160
talks began on adding environmental flow requirements to bilateral agreements.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:58.900
Some pilot releases from upstream reservoirs were tested,

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.720
yet these measures competed with agricultural and urban demands that had grown during

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:09.500
the period of expanded supply. New infrastructure proposals now include dedicated environmental

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:14.480
allocations, but they require renegotiations of long-standing delivery schedules.

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:21.060
The current state remains one of partial adaptation. De-salination continues to meet Israel's

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:27.060
internal needs while downstream ecological stress persists and requires ongoing diplomatic attention.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:33.460
Pilot releases of 30 to 50 million cubic meters in selected winters demonstrated that modest

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:39.440
freshets could lower salinity for several months, yet the same volume, if withheld from agriculture,

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:45.000
required compensatory desalinated supply whose energy cost fell on the Israeli side.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:50.920
Jordanian planners facing their own population growth resisted any reduction in the fixed

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:56.680
annual delivery quantity, even when the water arrived saltier than before. As a result,

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:02.200
the discussion has shifted toward joint financing of additional coastal plants whose output could

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:08.220
be earmarked for environmental flows, an approach that again treats supply expansion as the remedy for

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:14.140
a problem created by supply expansion. When Israel added coastal desalination capacity,

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:19.840
the policy rewarded reduced releases from the upper basin because the new water met demand

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:25.820
without those releases. Planners who measure only the volume delivered to users can miss the change

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:31.700
in return flow chemistry that follows. Later agreements that still treat water as a fixed

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:36.460
quantity rather than a variable in a connected system repeat the same blind spot.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:42.320
How might today's proposals for large-scale water projects account for the downstream chemistry

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:48.220
they will alter once the new supply is in place? That's the case for today. The lessons tend

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:52.660
to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd find it useful

